  "slug": "trees-phylogenetic-d93f",
  "summary": "Preprints target million-taxon species trees and massive CRISPR lineage phylogenies, demanding methods that scale without sacrificing statistical rigor.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is pairing formal guarantees or statistical consistency with explicit computational targets. QRAFT is a statistically consistent coalescent-based species tree method with near-linear time and linear memory, aimed at million-taxon trees. QUANTA recasts weighted quartet bipartitioning as a QUBO problem solved by quantum annealing on D-Wave hardware for species tree estimation. HERACLES optimizes hyperbolic cell embeddings under a quartet-based composite likelihood to reconstruct massive CRISPR lineage-recording phylogenies. PhytClust exactly optimizes monophyletic partitioning of rooted trees without a threshold and selects cluster number through a validity criterion. Ok-k-mer addresses distance estimation, selecting k-mer length ensembles that maximize Spearman correlation with phylogenetic branch distances. The items are methodologically heterogeneous, so this shared emphasis on scale and rigor is not a common algorithm.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-13"
}
